# Clown — second attempt at the bestiary's clown (the first lives on
# dispatch/ox-alpha-clown-character, authored against an older tree, and its
# owner's verdict was "creepy and oddly likeable but doesn't look really like
# the inspiration except maybe it has purple clothes"). What it got wrong is
# written into the numbers below:
#
#   * THE HEAD IS THE CHARACTER. Roughly HALF the standing height, one big
#     smooth pale ball. Attempt one gave the head 38% and it read as
#     big-forehead-chibi, not clown. This one declares height 1.00 with a
#     cranium of radius 0.235 — a 47% ball before the jaw rounds anything.
#
#   * THE NOSE MUST READ RED. All flesh still shares ONE material and the
#     face sheet only DARKENS (blob-face-sheet.ts), so red is out of reach
#     for .blob exactly the way attempt one found. The difference here: the
#     kit carries a RED SHELL over this bead, so the silhouette is flesh
#     (deformable, severable, raymarched) and the colour is polygon. See
#     clown-kit.wam's `nose` piece.
#
# Everything hard or striped or purple (cap, hair tufts, ruff, tunic,
# stockings, mitts, shoes) lives in clown-kit.wam; the flesh here is the pale
# ball, the round belly, thin limbs and THE SMILE GROOVE.
model clown
  # Crown lands at y 1.002 — see the skull note below for the arithmetic.
  height 1.00
  # humanoid | digitigrade. Checked, not decorative — see blob-checks.ts.
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # CHIBI STACK, shorter than attempt one (crotch 0.44 -> 0.36), because the
  # extra height budget went to the head: a 47% cranium plus a torso means
  # something else gives, and the reference gives it in the legs — they are
  # barely a quarter of the body under the tunic hem, and the huge shoes eat
  # most of what shows.
  root pelvis at 0.36 len=0.06

  # Torso chain: hip line at 0.42, chest top at 0.57 — the whole trunk is
  # 0.15 m under a 0.47 m head. That ratio IS the joke; do not rebalance it
  # without re-shooting the references.
  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=2 len=0.075
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=2 len=0.075

  # Short neck, tipped back slightly (-10). The goblin needed -17 to bring a
  # jutting snout level; this face is a flat forward-facing ball. Almost all
  # of the neck bar hides inside the cranium anyway — the ruff collar (kit)
  # owns whatever shows.
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-10 len=0.045

  # LONG skull bone relative to the head on purpose: facePrims centres the
  # cranium at t=0.45 along it, so the bone lifts the centre (and the crown)
  # without growing the head itself. Arithmetic, not taste: neck tail
  # y 0.614 + 0.45 x 0.34 x cos4deg = centre 0.767, crown = 0.767 + 0.235
  # (headHeight 1.0) = 1.002, which is the `height` above.
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-4  len=0.34

  mirror
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.030 dir=side  len=0.032
    # Arms: short and HELD OUT — tilt=32 splays the elbow past the belly the
    # way the reference holds them, forearms angling FORWARD (pitch=20) so
    # the white mitts sit in front of the tunic where they read. On a chibi
    # whose belly is nearly as wide as its shoulders there is no room below
    # the elbow unless the elbow itself is pushed out — same race as the
    # goblin's, on a shorter track.
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=-8 tilt=32 len=0.135
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=20 tilt=-6 len=0.115
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down             len=0.058
    # Legs: thigh FORWARD (+8), shin BACK (-10) — the signs the goblin
    # settled on after the kangaroo-knee incident, and the same reason: in
    # .blob a positive pitch always carries toward +z, so a humanoid knee
    # wants thigh-forward/shin-back, the opposite of WAM's convention.
    # Gentle angles because these legs are half the goblin's length; a
    # stronger bow reads knock-kneed at this scale.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis   dir=down tilt=6  pitch=8   side=0.044 len=0.175
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down tilt=-6 pitch=-10            len=0.165
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd                              len=0.095
  end

body
  # Torso: a short barrel, widest LOW (the belly ring sits near the top of
  # the pelvis bone). Deep in z like the goblin's — bulk taken where the arms
  # are not — but gentler, because this reads as a toy's pot belly, not a
  # wound. Every ring here is measured against by the kit's tunic; if one
  # moves, the tunic rings must be re-marched against it (see the kit header).
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.00 r=0.062 deep=1.15 blend=0.0045
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.95 r=0.075 deep=1.30 blend=0.0050
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.60 r=0.072 deep=1.25 blend=0.0050
  blob torso on chest  at=0.50 r=0.068 deep=1.10 blend=0.0042
  blob torso on chest  at=1.00 r=0.058 deep=0.95 blend=0.0040

  # Neck column. Almost entirely swallowed by the cranium above and the ruff
  # below; it exists to give the head cluster a flesh bridge into the chest
  # cap (both meet at the chest-top joint, y 0.570).
  bar head on neck from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.042 deep=0.95 blend=0.0016

  # THE NOSE — the whole character hangs on it reading.
  #
  # REACH, computed rather than eyeballed. The bead sits 109 mm below the
  # cranium axis — measured off the reference: nose centre y 0.658 on a ball
  # spanning 0.532..1.002, i.e. the reference wears its whole face LOW, chin
  # at the ruff — so the ellipsoid has curved away underneath it. Local
  # semi-depth at dy 0.109 is headRadius 0.235 x headDepth 0.92 x
  # sqrt(1-(0.109/0.235)^2) = 0.192. The bead's centre sits at z=0.178: only
  # 14 mm inside the local surface — but the bead's BACK cap is 69 mm in, so
  # it is attached through the gib pass while the neck stays narrow, because
  # a clown nose is a SEPARATE ball stuck on — and its front reaches z=0.233,
  # 41 mm PROUD of the face, half the bead's radius again, which is the
  # difference between a nose and a rash. Measured Ø on the reference is
  # ~0.11 of standing height, hence r 0.055 against the goblin-scale 0.048
  # this file started with.
  #
  # Same t=0.45 as
  # facePrims' own skull sphere so it tracks any future retune of HEAD_AT.
  # NOTE the z: offset= is relative to the BONE POINT, which itself sits at
  # z −0.0185 (the skull pitches back 4deg), so 0.196 here puts the bead
  # centre at world z 0.178 — an early draft wrote the world number as the
  # offset and lost 18 mm of reach silently.
  #
  # blend stays tight (0.002): a clown nose is a SEPARATE object stuck on —
  # the one part of this face that must not melt into the skull. Colour
  # note in the header: the RED lives on the kit's shell over this bead.
  blob head on skull at=0.45 r=0.055 blend=0.0020 offset=(0,-0.109,0.196)

  # THE SMILE — one thin hard-carved tube, a drawn arc. NOT a groove, and
  # that needs saying: the plan asked for `groove` (and the skill recommends
  # it), but grooves are currently DEAD in this engine — resolve.ts's
  # placePrims rebuilds each placed primitive field-by-field and drops
  # grooveDepth/grooveWidth, so sdBody and the packed shader both see zero
  # and no groove has ever cut anything (the shipped goblin's mouth crease
  # renders nothing; its mouth reads off the painted sheet alone). Reported
  # rather than routed around silently; when placePrims carries the fields,
  # this line can become `groove ... depth=0.004 width=0.0045` verbatim.
  #
  # Geometry — three attempts, each failure worth recording:
  #
  #   * The rescued draft used three LENTIL grooves (flat in y, like the
  #     goblin's mouth crease) hung 50 mm INSIDE the ball — a groove/carve
  #     cuts only where its own shell crosses the skin, so it carved
  #     nothing. Its test passed anyway, spuriously: the scan crossed the
  #     NOSE bead's blend, not a channel. No smile ever rendered.
  #   * Re-centring lentils on the skin still cuts at POINTS, not along a
  #     line: a lentil's zero-set is its rim plus its flat faces, and the
  #     rim crosses a near-spherical chin at up to four points. The
  #     goblin's lentil works because its muzzle is a near-CYLINDER whose
  #     curvature happens to match the rim's ellipse; a chin is not.
  #   * A straight tube chord sags inside the sphere; one quadratic over
  #     the full width lifts off the skin at its ends. The sphere outruns
  #     any single parabola.
  #
  # So: TWO mirrored half-arcs (one quadratic per half — a single parabola
  # over the full width cannot track the chin: the skin drops 13 mm between
  # x 0 and x 0.05 and no one k fits both ends), each a tube of r 4 mm
  # whose bezier is fitted to the MEASURED skin (sdBody bisection at 0/20/39/
  # 60/78 mm from the centre): axis from (0, 0.578, 0.109) at the middle to
  # (0.078, 0.585, 0.0735) at the corner, bulging to z 0.105 over the
  # quarter — i.e. the tube's outer edge rides the skin and its body sits
  # ~4-10 mm under it, a 12 mm-deep drawn line at the middle fading toward
  # the corners exactly as the reference's arc does. offset/tip/bend are
  # relative to the skull's t=0.45 bone point (0, 0.7669, −0.0133) — measured
  # off the compiled prim, not assumed; an early draft assumed −0.0107 and
  # the whole arc sat 2.6 mm shy of its skin. `hard`
  # for a crisp drawn edge; a smooth carve smears (face.ts's four-rebuild
  # lesson). (Not one mirrored part: `mirror` requires a PAIRED bone and
  # the skull is not one, so the left half is written out by hand.)
  carve on skull at=0.45 r=0.004 offset=(0,-0.189,0.1243) tip=(0.078,0.007,-0.0375) bend=(0,-0.001,0.0254) hard
  carve on skull at=0.45 r=0.004 offset=(0,-0.189,0.1243) tip=(-0.078,0.007,-0.0375) bend=(0,-0.001,0.0254) hard

  # ARMS AND LEGS — ball joints, deliberately, same stylisation as the
  # goblin: a fat nub at every joint kept readable by tight blends. On a
  # toy the JOINTS are most of the limb's charm, so they run proportionally
  # fatter than the goblin's relative to their shafts.
  blob arm on clavicle at=1.00 r=0.040 blend=0.0014 mirror
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.02 to=0.96 r=0.026 blend=0.0018 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=0.00 r=0.031 blend=0.0030 mirror
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.06 to=0.94 r=0.023 blend=0.0024 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=1.00 r=0.025 blend=0.0026 mirror

  # Mitten hands: one smooth blob, no fingers. A clown glove is a paddle;
  # the kit's white puff wraps this and reads the glove.
  blob arm on hand at=0.55 r=0.036 deep=0.95 blend=0.0034 mirror

  blob leg on thigh at=0.00 r=0.046 blend=0.0038 mirror
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.08 to=0.92 r=0.036 blend=0.0030 mirror
  blob leg on shin  at=0.00 r=0.040 blend=0.0036 mirror
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.08 to=0.92 r=0.030 blend=0.0028 mirror
  blob leg on shin  at=1.00 r=0.032 blend=0.0030 mirror

  # Feet: ankle nub, shaft forward, toe nub — mostly swallowed by the kit's
  # oversized shoes, but the shoes are open shells at the topline, so what
  # shows through has to be shaped like a foot.
  bar  leg on foot from=0.10 to=0.80 r=0.028 tall=0.80 blend=0.0028 mirror
  blob leg on foot at=0.90 r=0.032 wide=1.10 tall=0.70 deep=0.95 blend=0.0030 mirror

# A round, young skull — same direction as attempt one on every dial that
# matters, just BIGGER, because head size was the owner's first complaint.
# A round, young skull — a TALL OVAL rather than a ball: measured off the
# reference, the pale head is 0.47 of standing height TALL but only ~0.30
# WIDE, and the width of the silhouette belongs to the hair wings (±0.25),
# not to the cranium. The first cut of this file had headWidth 0.96 (a
# near-round 0.45-wide ball) and the render read as one giant blob with
# wings glued on. NO geometric brow (the arched brows are painted on the
# sheet — a heavy geometric brow is the scowl dial), no parameterised nose
# at all (noseLength disabled — the bead above IS the nose), and a
# vestigial jaw that only rounds off the bottom.
face
  headRadius 0.235
  headWidth  0.70
  headHeight 1.00
  headDepth  0.92
  jawWidth   0.78
  jawHeight  0.84
  jawDrop    0.050
  jawJut     0.010
  noseLength 0.0004
  browHeavy  0.0004
  headBlend  0.0030

# The clown's own face. The projection is PLANAR over the head's axes
# (march.wgsl: raw = (hs.x, hs.y), u/v = -1..1 across the ball's width and
# height), so every value below is a fraction of the head's PROJECTED size —
# narrowing headWidth to 0.70 shrank the sheet's metres-per-unit by 30%,
# which is why eyeGap and eyeSize had to GROW to keep the same face on a
# narrower head: measured on the reference, the eyes are 0.09 of standing
# height wide and sit 0.20 apart, centred 0.02 BELOW the ball's equator —
# eyeRise 0.545, not the 0.42 this file started with. At 0.42 the eyes
# landed at y 0.805, jammed under the cap band (bottom edge 0.809), which
# is exactly the "cap swallowed the face" read the owner rejected on
# attempt one: no forehead, eyes fused into the band.
# eyeGlow is LOW on purpose: the sheet mixes the eye toward this value and
# the shader's emissive mask keys BRIGHT texels, so 1.0 would light two
# lanterns; 0.14 is a dark charcoal that still reads against a lit
# forehead. WHAT THE FORMAT CANNOT DO: the white highlight inside each eye.
# Only the eye region brightens, toward ONE flat value — there is no second
# bright spot inside a dark feature, so the glint is dropped, not faked.
sheet
  # The reference's eyes are enormous and reach almost to the head's sides:
  # centres ±0.10 m (0.61 of the sheet's half-width), Ø ~0.09 m.
  eyeGap     0.60
  eyeSize    0.135
  # Near-BLACK. The sheet is an albedo MULTIPLIER (tex/mean), so the eye's
  # brightness is eyeGlow/0.44 of the flesh around it: 0.06 gives a 7:1
  # contrast, which is what a drawn eye on whiteface wants. The first cut
  # of this pass used 0.14 and the LIT eye faded to near-invisible against
  # the cheek — measured on render, luminance 130-180 against a 250 face.
  # Not zero: zero leaves the eye at base grey and it stops reading as a
  # drawn eye at all.
  eyeGlow    0.06
  # NOTE: the drawer hardcodes its 1.25 squash (blob-face-sheet.ts draws
  # hypot(dx, dy * 1.25)); this key is accepted but inert today. Eyes will
  # run slightly WIDE of the reference's tall ovals until the drawer reads
  # the parameter.
  eyeSquash  0.80
  eyePupil   0
  # 0.545 puts the eye centres at y 0.746 — 21 mm BELOW the ball's equator,
  # measured off the reference, leaving a bare forehead (y 0.81-0.95)
  # between the cap band and the brows. This is the single number that
  # fixes "the cap swallowed the face".
  eyeRise    0.545
  eyeTilt    0
  # Thin, gently ARCHED brows — browAngle negative lifts the inner ends,
  # where positive (the zombie's scowl) drops them. Kept light: any heavier
  # and the innocence goes.
  browHeavy  0.38
  browAngle  -8
  mouthWidth 0.32
  # The painted smile sits LOW with the groove underneath (both measured to
  # y ~0.57-0.58, just above the chin where the ruff begins): v 0.85 on this
  # ball. The old 0.70 painted it mid-cheek while the groove cut at the
  # chin — two mouths 80 mm apart, the exact drift the note below warns of.
  mouthRise  0.85
  # POSITIVE — a smile, matching the grooves underneath. The paint leads,
  # the groove gives it shadow; if the two drift apart on render, trust the
  # render and re-site whichever is wrong (the goblin's shades lesson).
  mouthCurve 0.35
  # Barely open — the GROOVE is the smile's geometry; this is its paint.
  mouthOpen  0.018
  nostril    0.15
  noseRidge  0.0
  noseWide   0.0
  noseLong   0.0
  # Lighter than the goblin's: the mouth paint now sits where this band
  # lands (mouthRise + 0.135 = 0.985, the sheet's very bottom edge), and a
  # full-strength jaw shade there smeared a dark beard under the smile.
  jawShade   0.18
  # Toy-smooth: less than the old clown even, because blotching reads as
  # decay next to the zombie and this character is stage makeup.
  grain      0.05
  seed       11

# Pale PINK-cream skin, matte like painted vinyl. Retuned off the measured
# reference this pass: the face paint there is #e8c2bf — linear (0.80, 0.55,
# 0.53) — distinctly pinker than the pure cream this file started with
# (which rendered (249,218,176) against the reference's (232,194,191)).
# fresnelBoost lifted 0.40 -> 0.55: the head is a big sphere and its
# shadowed flank was falling to near-black against the lab's dark
# background, which read as a HOLE in the silhouette beside the cap — the
# "front view still suggests a gap" note from the rescue. The wings now
# flank that zone too, but the rim light is what actually keeps it.
#
# Linear RGB, sRGB-encoded on output — these render lighter and pinker than
# the raw numbers suggest. The mottle is ROSY at low amplitude: faint warmth
# in the cheeks and knuckles, never dirt.
palette
  baseColor     0.88 0.73 0.60
  deepColor     0.55 0.18 0.14
  charColor     0.10 0.08 0.07
  specIntensity 0.45
  specRoughness 0.50
  fresnelBoost  0.55
  translucency  0.25
  wetness       0.30
  mottleAmp     0.30
  mottleScale   1.6
  mottleColor   0.62 0.24 0.20
